    <h1>About</h1>

    <p>This blog explores <strong>AI safety and alignment</strong> — the problem of making increasingly capable AI systems reliably do what we intend, not just what we literally ask for. It's the defining technical challenge of our era, and understanding it is essential for anyone building or deploying AI systems.</p>

    <p>I write about:</p>

    <p><strong>Alignment research</strong> — outer and inner alignment, specification gaming, reward hacking, alignment faking, mechanistic interpretability.<br>
    <strong>Safety techniques</strong> — RLHF, Constitutional AI, red teaming, debate-based oversight, scalable supervision.<br>
    <strong>Real-world deployment</strong> — how alignment failures manifest in production systems and what practitioners can do about it.<br>
    <strong>Frontier developments</strong> — analysis of new papers and results from Anthropic, DeepMind, OpenAI, and academic labs.</p>

    <p>Every article is grounded in published research with direct citations to primary sources.</p>

    <p>I've been building AI systems for over a decade — shipping models, fine-tuning LLMs, deploying agents in enterprise environments, and conducting applied research across generative AI, computer vision, and agentic systems. I hold multiple patents in AI and have published research on AI safety, low-resource language AI, and enterprise AI deployment.</p>

    <p>This blog is my way of thinking in public about the problem I believe matters most.</p>

